+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard must show committed stall-status changes in up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers, with a hard goal of 15 seconds under normal connectivity and bursts around opening time. How committed changes reach browsers is load-bearing for three design nodes at once — change store, delivery, and browser sync are all sized around whichever delivery mechanism is chosen — so the choice must be made before those nodes are written.
+
+Three delivery mechanisms are in the domain. All three can meet the 15-second goal; subsecond delivery would be useful but is not required. That floor is background, not a criterion: it does not separate the options.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Vendor independence (major)** — whether change delivery depends on a third-party service's pricing, protocol, or availability. A recurring fee or a provider coupling outlives any single market weekend and constrains every later change to the delivery path.
+- **Update latency (moderate)** — how soon a committed change appears in a connected browser. The 15-second goal is the floor; faster delivery directly serves opening-time bursts, when coordinators are watching the board closely.
+- **Operations owned by this project (moderate)** — connection capacity, session state, and reconnect handling the project must run itself. Less of this is preferable, but it is in-house work that can be absorbed and iterated on.
+- **Server read load at 800 browsers (minor)** — steady request volume the delivery route generates regardless of how many edits occur.
+
+No gates: the freshness floor and the HLD's tenet that self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted are satisfied by every option in contention, so neither eliminates one. That tenet deliberately leaves this choice open — no tenet picks a winner, so selection rests on the criteria above.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers re-request the board — a full snapshot or changes since a cursor — over ordinary HTTPS every five seconds. The server answers each request independently and keeps no per-browser connection state; the change store answers cursor queries ("what has changed since revision N"); delivery stays plain request/response; each browser runs its own polling loop.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Vendor independence — **strong**: no third-party service in the path.
+- Update latency — **weak**: a committed change waits up to five seconds plus request round-trip before it can appear; a burst around opening time reads as a staggered trickle.
+- Operations owned — **strong**: no connection capacity to manage and no reconnect protocol; a failed request simply retries on the next tick.
+- Server read load — **weak**: 800 browsers polling every five seconds is a steady ~160 requests per second whether or not anything changed.
+
+**Summary.** Simplest stateless operation, paid for with repeated reads and seconds of lag.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** Browsers hold a long-lived WebSocket to infrastructure this project runs. The server pushes each committed change to connected clients immediately. The change store keeps replayable revisions so a client — fresh, or returning after a disconnect of up to 20 minutes — can catch up from the last revision it saw. The delivery node owns sessions, capacity, heartbeats, and publication; browser sync owns reconnect and catch-up.
+
+**Demonstration.** A committed edit lands as revision N; the server pushes `{revision: N}` on each open socket. A browser reconnecting with "last seen: N-3" receives revisions N-2 through N and resumes live.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Vendor independence — **strong**: no third-party service, fee, or protocol coupling in the delivery path.
+- Update latency — **strong**: push at commit time.
+- Operations owned — **weak**: the project runs connection capacity and owns reconnect behavior end to end.
+- Server read load — **strong**: updates flow out once per commit; catch-up reads occur only around disconnects.
+
+**Summary.** Fast updates without a realtime vendor bill; the cost is connection and reconnect operations run in-house.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** The server publishes committed changes to a third-party realtime provider; browsers subscribe through the provider's SDK and subscription model. The change store must reconcile publication (confirm the provider holds what the server committed) and carry provider credentials; delivery wraps the provider's API rather than managing sockets directly.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Vendor independence — **weak**: recurring fees, and the board's delivery path is coupled to a provider's pricing, protocol, and account status.
+- Update latency — **strong**: the provider exists to push low-latency updates.
+- Operations owned — **partial**: the provider absorbs connection capacity and much of the reconnect machinery, but credentials, publication reconciliation, and provider incidents remain in-house.
+- Server read load — **strong**: no polling loop on the browsers.
+
+**Summary.** Quickest route to low-latency updates with fewer connection operations, at the price of fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.** It matches the hosted service on latency and beats polling on it, and it is the only fast option that also scores strong on the one major criterion, vendor independence. Hosted realtime scores weak on that major criterion; that outweighs its strong latency, which self-hosted matches anyway. Polling remains defensible on operations and read simplicity but gives up the speed the board wants exactly when coordinators watch it most — opening-time bursts.
+
+The decision commits the project to the weak fit on operations owned: connection capacity and reconnect handling become this project's problem, carried by the delivery and browser-sync nodes. It forecloses a realtime vendor bill and a provider protocol from the delivery path, and obligates the change store to keep replayable revisions with reliable publication so reconnecting browsers can catch up.
